---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PostLink from '../components/PostLink.astro';
import { ASSETS, INSIGHTS_ENABLED, SITE } from '../config.ts';
import { getInsightPosts } from '../lib/insights.ts';

// The Insights post each homepage section links to, by file name. Every
// link shows the post's title and reading time, and all four are left out
// while Insights is switched off. Renaming, removing, or drafting one of
// these posts stops the build with a message naming it.
const SECTION_POSTS = {
  problem: 'more-capable-less-predictable',
  whatWeDo: 'measures-records-bounds',
  howItWorks: 'safety-that-does-not-cost-throughput',
  whyNow: 'robotics-is-where-aviation-was-in-1988',
} as const;

const posts = INSIGHTS_ENABLED ? await getInsightPosts() : [];
const sectionPost = (id: string) => {
  const post = posts.find((candidate) => candidate.id === id);
  if (INSIGHTS_ENABLED && !post) {
    throw new Error(
      `The homepage links to the Insights post "${id}", but no published post has that file name.`,
    );
  }
  return post;
};

const problemPost = sectionPost(SECTION_POSTS.problem);
const whatWeDoPost = sectionPost(SECTION_POSTS.whatWeDo);
const howItWorksPost = sectionPost(SECTION_POSTS.howItWorks);
const whyNowPost = sectionPost(SECTION_POSTS.whyNow);
---

<Base title={SITE.title} description={SITE.description}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <Hero />

    <Section id="problem" heading="The problem" variant="tinted">
      <p>
        Humanoids, cobots, and autonomous robots are moving out of industrial
        cages and into shared spaces around people. Their behavior is
        increasingly driven by AI, rather than by hand-coded logic.
      </p>
      <p>
        As these robots take on continuously improving artificial brains, they
        become more capable but less predictable in what they do. Without ways
        to bound what robots do, risk is hard to contain, accountability is
        difficult to establish, and deployment of AI-driven robots around
        people slows. If the way to contain that risk is to slow the robot
        down, safety costs throughput every second the robot moves.
      </p>
      {problemPost && <PostLink post={problemPost} />}
    </Section>

    <Section id="what-we-do" heading="What we do" class="!pb-8 md:!pb-12">
      <p>
        We are building runtime safety software for AI-driven robots. The
        software sits at the execution boundary between the brain and the
        body, where decisions become motion. At that boundary, the software
        measures, records, and bounds what the robot does, regardless of
        what the AI decides or how it evolves.
      </p>
      {whatWeDoPost && <PostLink post={whatWeDoPost} />}
      <div class="page-bleed mt-6 md:mt-8 mb-0">
        <picture>
          <!-- 4:5 portrait, brand-accent strokes on transparent bg, for phones. -->
          <source
            media="(max-width: 639px)"
            srcset={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.narrow.src}
            width={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.narrow.width}
            height={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.narrow.height}
          />
          <!-- Default: 16:9 landscape, sized for tablet + desktop. -->
          <img
            src={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.wide.src}
            alt={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.alt}
            width={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.wide.width}
            height={ASSETS.boundaryFigure.wide.height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="w-full h-auto"
          />
        </picture>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="how-it-works" heading="How it works" variant="tinted">
      <p>
        The software keeps the AI in control by handling the millisecond
        physics, so safety does not cost throughput.
      </p>
      <p>
        Anti-lock brakes do the same for a driver. Braking hard can lock the
        wheels, which then slide and cannot steer. ABS handles the millisecond
        physics the driver cannot, so the wheels keep rolling, the car stays
        steerable, and the driver can get around the car ahead. Anti-lock
        brakes do not prevent the collision; they keep the driver in control
        to avoid it.
      </p>
      {howItWorksPost && <PostLink post={howItWorksPost} />}
    </Section>

    <Section id="why-now" heading="Why now">
      <p>
        Aviation, automotive, and computing each reached a moment when
        execution had to be separated from decision-making. In each case the
        reason was the same: the safety claim could no longer rest on the
        system making the decisions. Robotics is at the same moment.
      </p>
      {whyNowPost && <PostLink post={whyNowPost} />}
    </Section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
